{
  "id": "partizip-als-adjektiv",
  "name": "-ed and -ing adjectives (bored / boring)",
  "level": "B1",
  "subtitle": "Use -ed to say how a person feels: I'm bored. Use -ing for the thing that causes the feeling: The film is boring. Same verb, two different adjectives.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "-ed",
    "-ing",
    "bored",
    "boring"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "person or thing"
      },
      {
        "text": "-ed = how someone feels",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "-ing = what causes the feeling",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I'm <span class=\"vp-verb\">bored</span>. · The film is <span class=\"vp-prep\">boring</span>. · She's <span class=\"vp-verb\">interested</span> in art. · Art is <span class=\"vp-prep\">interesting</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>-ed</strong> says <em>how a person feels</em>: <em>I'm tired. He was excited. We're interested in the course.</em>",
        "tag": "-ed: the feeling"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>-ing</strong> says <em>what causes the feeling</em>: a thing, a person or a situation. <em>The film is boring. The news was exciting. The journey was tiring.</em>",
        "tag": "-ing: the cause"
      },
      {
        "html": "You can use both before a noun: <em>a bored child</em> (he feels bored), <em>a boring lesson</em> (it makes people bored). Be careful with people: <em>He is bored</em> = he feels bored. <em>He is boring</em> = other people find him boring.",
        "tag": "Before a noun, and with people"
      },
      {
        "html": "Spelling: if the verb ends in <em>-e</em>, drop the <em>e</em>: <em>excite → excited, exciting</em>. If it ends in consonant + <em>y</em>: <em>worry → worried, worrying</em>.",
        "tag": "Spelling"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>I'm very boring.</em> (when you feel bored) → ✓ <em>I'm very bored.</em> · ✗ <em>The film was bored.</em> → ✓ <em>The film was boring.</em> · ✗ <em>I'm interesting in music.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm interested in music.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Most of these adjectives come from verbs about feelings: <em>to bore, to interest, to excite, to surprise</em>. The film <em>bores</em> you, so the film is <em>boring</em> and you are <em>bored</em>. Some adjectives take a fixed preposition: <em>interested in, excited about, worried about, surprised by, tired of</em>. More about prepositions in <a href=\"/verbs-prepositions\">Verbs and prepositions</a>. When you use several adjectives before a noun, see <a href=\"/adjective-order\">Order of adjectives</a>: <em>a boring old film</em>.",
    "reference": {
